    id: 'rc-b', section: 'verbal', title: 'Blank spaces',
    text: 'For a long time, mapmakers filled what they did not know. Sea monsters curled in empty oceans; the interiors of continents were crowded with invented rivers, kingdoms and mountains copied from older maps. In the eighteenth century a different habit spread among some French cartographers: they began to leave unknown regions blank. One celebrated map of Africa removed decades of imagined detail and showed the interior as white space, with only coasts and confirmed rivers drawn. The change was not simply a matter of honesty. A blank was an invitation and a claim. It told readers that the continent was knowable, that it had not yet been known by Europeans, and that someone ought to go and find out, which, in practice, often meant that someone ought to go and take. Explorers\' accounts from the following century describe these blank spaces as irresistible, and historians have since pointed out that the people living in them were not blank to themselves. The white space was a property of the map, not of the land. Modern maps keep the habit in another form. Satellite images seem to show everything, yet what a map displays is chosen: which roads are named, which settlements are labelled, which borders are drawn as disputed. A map can still be blank without being white.',
